{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.MonodromyDatumRigidity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.MonodromyDatumRigidity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.CyclicDilogPentagon"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MonodromyDatumRigidity.qpRatio_solution_independent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MonodromyDatumRigidity.qpRatio_solution_independent","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MonodromyDatumRigidity.lean#L103-L122","doc":"**The quasi-period readout is already solution-independent** (no pentagon\nneeded): for two `FaddeevPhi` solutions at `b`, wherever both are nonzero the\nratio `z ↦ Φ(z + ib)/Φ(z)` agrees, because `phi_b_quasiperiod` fixes it to the\ncommon quasi-period factor `1 + q_b·e^{2πbz}`. This is the *local* half; the\npentagon input of `monodromy_datum_rigidity` is what extends invariance to the\n*dual / large-argument* monodromy the local quasi-period does not pin. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MonodromyDatumRigidity.qpRatio_scaleInvariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MonodromyDatumRigidity.qpRatio_scaleInvariant","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MonodromyDatumRigidity.lean#L94-L101","doc":"**Concrete scale-invariant readout — the quasi-period ratio.** The functional\n`Ψ ↦ (z ↦ Ψ(z + ib)/Ψ(z))` is scale-invariant: a nonzero constant cancels in the\nquotient. A non-vacuous witness that `ScaleInvariant` functionals exist. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MonodromyDatumRigidity.monodromy_datum_rigidity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MonodromyDatumRigidity.monodromy_datum_rigidity","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MonodromyDatumRigidity.lean#L72-L92","doc":"**The monodromy datum is a pentagon-rigidity invariant.** Assuming pentagon\nrigidity (`PentagonRigidity b Φ`, the L4 open input) and given two nowhere-zero\n`FaddeevPhi` solutions at `b`, **every** scale-invariant readout `D` takes the\n*same value* on both. The bar-odd datum `A_ω` read off any `Φ_b` representative\nis therefore one pentagon-forced invariant — no fitting freedom.\n\nDerivation: rigidity gives `Φᵢ = cᵢ·Φ` pointwise; nowhere-vanishing forces\n`cᵢ ≠ 0`; scale-invariance collapses both readouts to `D Φ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MonodromyDatumRigidity.ScaleInvariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MonodromyDatumRigidity.ScaleInvariant","kind":"def","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MonodromyDatumRigidity.lean#L65-L70","doc":"**Scale-invariant readout.** A functional `D` of a candidate solution that\nis unchanged when the solution is rescaled by a nonzero constant. A monodromy /\nStokes datum — a ratio of analytic continuations — is of this form: the overall\nnormalisation of `Φ_b` cancels. ","signatureChunk":0}]}